Q: how Spring creates an object using the reflection if the class has only one arg constructor A: <p>As noted in <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79932704/6395627">Sina Soheili's answer</a>, Spring supports constructor and setter injection in addition to field injection. In fact, it's best practice to avoid field injection. But they all make use of reflection. Reflection can be used to invoke constructors and methods. It's not just about fields.</p>
<p>For a direct example (without Spring), you can create an instance of <code>B</code> yourself via reflection like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public static class A {}

public static class B {

  private final A a;

  public B(A a) {
    this.a = a;
  }

  public A getA() {
    return a;
  }
}

void main() throws ReflectiveOperationException {
  A a = A.class.getConstructor().newInstance();
  B b = B.class.getConstructor(A.class).newInstance(a);
  IO.println(&quot;b   = &quot; + b);
  IO.println(&quot;b.a = &quot; + b.getA());
}
</code></pre>
<p>Copy that code into a source file named <code>Example.java</code> and execute it<sup>1</sup>. You'll see something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>&gt; java Example.java
b   = Example$B@62e136d3
b.a = Example$A@6279cee3
</code></pre>
<p>Now, Spring is much more complicated because it needs to take into account annotations, contexts, scopes, lifecycles, producers, multiple implementations, arbitrary code, and so on. But at a fundamental level the above example demonstrates how Spring can create an instance of a class via reflection even if that class doesn't have a no-argument constructor.</p>
<p>As an aside, if a class has only one constructor then you don't need to annotate it with <code>@Autowired</code>. Spring assumes that one constructor is the one that's meant to be called (as obviously there's no other choice).</p>
<hr />
<p><sup>1. The example uses the <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/IO.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>java.lang.IO</code></a> class added in Java 25. Executing it also makes use of <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/330" rel="nofollow noreferrer">JEP 330: Launch Single-File Source-Code Programs</a> and <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/512" rel="nofollow noreferrer">JEP 512: Compact Source Files and Instance Main Methods</a>, added in Java 11 and Java 25, respectively. So the example requires Java 25+ to run as-is. However, the reflection code should be applicable even in Java 1.1 (released 1997).</sup></p>

Q: Trying to create a source alias or function to the PowerShell . builtin A: <p>You're better off with a module if you can afford it but this seems to work for me and my purposes. I'm only sourcing scripts into a top level script. Whether it works beyond that is up for debate.</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>function Import {
    [CmdletBinding()]
    param(
        [Parameter(Mandatory)]
        [string] $Path
    )

    process {
        $Files = Get-ChildItem -Path $Path -File |
            Where-Object { $_.Extension -in @('.ps1', '.psd1') }

        foreach ($File in $Files) {
            Write-Debug &quot;Importing: $($File.FullName)&quot;
            $ExecutionContext.InvokeCommand.InvokeScript(
                &lt;# UseLocalScope #&gt; $PSCmdlet.SessionState,
                &lt;# string script #&gt; { . $File.FullName },
                &lt;# IList input #&gt; $null,
                &lt;# Params System.Object[] args #&gt; $null
            )
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Why does Python not call the function descriptor inside this other descriptor? A: <p>The problem is simply that in your snippet, the decorated method is assigned as the descriptor <code>value</code> attribute, but when <code>__get__</code> is called, it is returned unchanged: that is you don't call the original <code>__get__</code> which is what performs the &quot;method binding&quot; by returning the equivalent of a <code>partial</code> function call.</p>
<p>Just to say it in other words: what does create a &quot;bound method&quot; is the code inside <code>FunctionType.__get__</code>   -  there is no special case for bound methods but the descriptor protocol being used in the function itself, and returning an object that is functionally equivalent to calling the function with <code>functools.partial</code> with the &quot;<code>self</code>&quot; parameter. WHen you do <code>self.value</code> inside the descriptor class, you are using normal attribute access to get an ordinary instance attribute, not going through the language descriptor mechanisms - which require a class attribute to feature the <code>__get__</code> method.</p>
<p>If you want it to be bound to the owner instance, you have to do that yourself.  If your goal is to decorate functions this way, you'd better just leave <code>__set__</code> without any implementation, so that you have a <code>non data</code> descriptor (just like regular methods behave in Python):</p>
<pre><code>from functools import partial


class Identity:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value

    def __get__(self, obj, objtype=None):
        if obj is None:
            return self
        # Here is the missing part: what causes the method
        # to be bound is the original call to the function's own `__get__`
        return self.value.__get__(obj, objttype)

        # That is equivalent to :
        # return partial(self.value, obj) : i.e. the &quot;self&quot; 
        # first parameter is annotated into the bound object and will
        # be lazily inserted into the method when it is finally called.


    def __repr__(self):
        return f&quot;Descriptor Identity, wrapping {self.value}&quot;

class Example:
    @Identity
    def non_method():
        pass



</code></pre>
<p>(On a side note: when experimenting with these mechanisms, and trying to investigate what is called and what is not, I recommend just writing in some side effects into the code you are not sure is running itself: in other words, add some <code>print</code> calls into the <code>__get__</code> (and when you get to it) the <code>__set__</code> and <code>__set_name__</code> methods: it will make your understanding much easier)</p>
<p>Your code works the &quot;decorator&quot; side of it ok - but it is good to keep in mind that the decorator behavior is not inherent to a descriptor.
So, just to recap that:
People frequently  mistake what the descriptor protocol does, with the features that the built-in <code>property</code> provides.</p>
<p>While <code>property</code> does indeed works like you try to do there, it does so <em>not</em> because it is a descriptor, but because it is coded to work like a decorator, and then wiring up its own descriptor &quot;dunder&quot; methods to work through the methods that were decorated.</p>
<p>The descriptor protocol states that if an instance (of, say, class <code>Identity</code>) that is the value of a <em>class</em> (say, of class <code>Example</code>) attribute features any of the <code>__get__</code>, <code>__set__</code> or <code>__delete__</code> methods it will work as a descriptor: instances of class <code>Example</code> will go through the descriptor methods for reading, writing or deleting the attribute, and accessing the attribute of the class <code>Example</code> itself will work even when reading the attribute from the <code>Example</code> class.</p>
<p>The <em>decorator</em>  is a separate thing: it is a syntax that calls the decorator callable with the class or function that is decorated as first positional parameter, and replaces that function (or class) in the same namespace with whatever the decorator callable returns.</p>
<p><code>property</code> creates a class that do both things, which you don't do in your <code>Identity</code> class. If you are to use a class as a decorator, the second argument (after <code>self</code> passed to the class <code>__init__</code> is the decorated method. The instance of <code>Identity</code> itself then replaces that method in the class body.</p>

Q: Why is the onDrag closure called after dropping an item in SwiftUI? A: <p>I got around this issue by tracking the drag state:</p>
<pre><code>enum DragState {
    case dragging, complete, ready

    var isReady: Bool {
        self == .ready
    }

    var isComplete: Bool {
        self == .complete
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>.onDrag:</p>
<pre><code>.onDrag {
    if dragState.isReady {
        let _ = print(&quot;ContentView &gt; Ready to Drag&quot;)
        self.draggedItem = item
        dragState = .dragging
        return NSItemProvider(object: item.id.uuidString as NSString)
    }

    if dragState.isComplete {
        let _ = print(&quot;ContentView &gt; Switching to Ready&quot;)
        dragState = .ready
    }

    return NSItemProvider()
}
</code></pre>
<p>Delegate:</p>
<pre><code>func performDrop(info: DropInfo) -&gt; Bool {
        draggedItem = nil
        dragState = .complete
        return true
    }
</code></pre>
